    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0261",
      "sequence": 273,
      "meaning": "Seeing Vaidehī’s immense distress—each instant passing for her like an age—Rāma reflects that a pond of nectar does nothing for one who has already died of thirst, rain does nothing after every crop has dried, and regret is useless once the proper time is lost. Understanding this inwardly, he looks toward Jānakī; seeing her special love, the Lord thrills. He mentally bows to his guru and, with astonishing lightness, raises the bow. It flashes like lightning as he takes it up and then curves like a rainbow in the sky. No one standing there perceives the acts of taking, stringing and drawing it taut; in that very instant Rāma snaps the bow at its middle, and a terrible, hard sound fills the worlds.",
