    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0263",
      "sequence": 275,
      "meaning": "Cymbals, mṛdaṅgas, conches, shawms, kettledrums and drums sound beautifully; many fine instruments play and young women sing auspicious songs everywhere. The queen rejoices intensely with Sītā’s friends, like water falling on drying grain. Janaka leaves worry behind and finds happiness like a swimmer who, exhausted, has touched bottom. The kings become lustreless when the bow breaks, as lamps lose brilliance by day. Who could describe Sītā’s happiness? She is like a thirsty cātaka receiving a drop of Svāti rain. Lakṣmaṇa gazes at Rāma as a young cakora gazes at the moon. Śatānanda then gives permission, and Sītā goes toward Rāma with her beautiful, clever companions singing auspicious verses, moving with the grace of a young swan and immeasurable beauty in every limb.",
