    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0265",
      "sequence": 277,
      "meaning": "Music sounds in city and sky; the wicked grow pale and all the good rejoice. Gods, Kinnaras, humans, Nāgas and great sages cry victory again and again and offer blessings. Heavenly women dance and sing, repeatedly releasing handfuls of flowers. Brahmins sound Vedic chants everywhere, while heralds recite formal praises. Rāma’s fame spreads through earth, the netherworld and heaven: he has chosen Sītā and broken the bow. The city’s men and women wave lamps and give away wealth without counting it. The pair of Sītā and Rāma shines as though beauty and adornment had gathered in one place. Her friends tell Sītā to clasp the Lord’s feet, but she is too afraid to touch them: remembering what happened to Gautama’s wife when those feet touched stone, she keeps her hands away. The jewel of Raghu’s line smiles inwardly, recognizing her extraordinary love.",
